// AUTO-GENERATED verbatim bundle of the seo-geo-prompt-set-architect skill (SKILL.md + references +
// template), concatenated in runner order. Regenerate if the skill changes. Do not edit by hand.
export const PROMPT_SET_ARCHITECT_BUNDLE = "===== FILE: SKILL.md =====\n---\nname: seo-geo-prompt-set-architect\ndescription: >-\n  Turns a website, competitors, and keyword or SEO metric data into a prioritised, deduplicated set\n  of non branded prompts for AI visibility and GEO, organised into three named campaigns: Citation\n  Commercial (commercial prompts won with the client's own pages, to earn the citation and the lead),\n  Mentions (commercial prompts won only by outreach onto the third party sources AI cites, like best\n  of listicles, directories, and Reddit), and Citation Information (low hanging informational prompts\n  won with owned content that feed the two commercial campaigns). Mirrors how Profound, Ahrefs, and\n  Semrush split content versus PR and citations versus mentions. Use whenever someone wants to build\n  or choose which prompts to track for AI visibility or GEO, split prompts into content versus\n  outreach campaigns, decide whether to out create or pitch, or map keywords to AI prompts.\n  Trigger for: \"build me a prompt set\", \"which prompts should I track\", \"citation vs mentions\n  campaign\", or \"prompts for Brand Radar or Profound\".\n---\n\n# SEO / GEO Prompt Set Architect\n\nYou take a website, its competitors, and whatever keyword or SEO metric data is available, and you build a prioritised, deduplicated set of non branded prompts, organised into three named campaigns that each carry a clear goal and a clear action. This is the instrument you measure AI share of voice with, and the plan for how to win each prompt.\n\nThis skill sits one level above `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst`, which works a single query at a time and cannot decide what to track. This skill decides what to track, in what order, and by which campaign. It sits beneath the brand level `seo-geo-expert` and alongside the rest of the suite.\n\nTwo truths to internalise:\n\n1. **A tracked prompt is not a keyword.** A keyword is a compressed search string; a prompt is the fuller, conversational question a buyer types into a chatbot. Translate every keyword into that form.\n2. **Winning a query is either an out create job or a pitch job.** For some commercial queries the client can win the answer by publishing their own page (out create). For most commercial category queries the answer is built from independent third party sources, so the only way in is outreach to get mentioned on those sources (pitch). Around 85 to 93 percent of commercial category citations are third party, and the single most cited content format is the ranked best of listicle. Deciding out create versus pitch for every prompt is the core of this skill, and it is exactly the split the major tools use (Profound separates a Content and AEO layer from a PR and Communications layer; trackers frame each source as \"outreach or outrank\").\n\n---\n\n## RULE 0: NO EM DASHES, NO EN DASHES (HARD CONSTRAINT)\n\nEvery deliverable follows the house style. Never use an em dash or an en dash anywhere, including ranges. Use a comma, colon, parentheses, a full stop, or the word \"to\" for ranges. Scan for both dash characters before returning anything and replace every instance. Verify with a Python Unicode check (count U+2014 and U+2013), not grep.\n\n---\n\n## The three campaigns (the core model)\n\nEvery prompt in the set belongs to exactly one campaign. The campaign name is the classification, the priority, and the action, all in one. There are no other codes. Build the campaigns in this order.\n\n### Campaign 1: Citation Commercial (priority one, the lead engine)\n\n- **What:** non branded commercial prompts the client can win with a page on their own site, so the client's own URL is the cited source in the answer.\n- **Why first:** these are the decision influencing queries. Winning them puts the client's own page in the answer at the moment of purchase intent, the most direct path to a lead.\n- **How to win (out create):** publish or optimise owned pages (comparison, use case, pricing, FAQ, service), answer first, with sourced data and clean, extractable structure.\n- **Tool parallel:** Profound's Content and AEO layer; the \"create AI optimised content\" action; the \"out create\" side of \"outreach or outrank\".\n\n### Campaign 2: Mentions (priority two, the outreach engine)\n\n- **What:** non branded commercial prompts where the answer is built from independent third party sources, so an owned page cannot win. You earn a brand mention on the pages the engine already cites.\n- **Why second:** high value but slower, because it needs outreach, not publishing. Most broad best of queries live here.\n- **How to win (pitch):** earn inclusion on the ranked best of listicles and comparison roundups the engine cites for the query; get listed and earn genuine reviews on the directories that lift citation odds (G2, Clutch, Capterra, Trustpilot); participate authentically in the Reddit and Quora threads and industry press the engine pulls from.\n- **Tool parallel:** Profound's PR and Communications layer (\"shape AI conversations\"); the \"earn mentions on the sources AI already trusts\" action; the \"outreach\" side of \"outreach or outrank\".\n\n### Campaign 3: Citation Information (priority three, the low hanging foundation)\n\n- **What:** non branded informational prompts the client wins with owned explainer content, so the client's own page is cited on the questions buyers ask while learning.\n- **Why it supports:** these are the easiest to win (low competition). They build the entity association and citation precedence that make the two commercial campaigns work, and focused discoverability here converts into commercial demand over time. Run it in parallel as the foundation.\n- **How to win (out create):** publish owned explainer and definitional content, answer first, sourced, structured for extraction.\n- **Tool parallel:** the informational content foundation; building citation precedence so the brand is already a trusted source when the commercial question is asked.\n\n**The one decision that assigns Campaign 1 versus Campaign 2 is out create or pitch:** can the client win this with their own page, or only by getting onto someone else's. Read the live citation landscape to decide (see `references/campaigns.md`). Informational prompts go to Campaign 3. A small number of commercial prompts need both an owned page and outreach; run them in both campaigns and say so plainly.\n\n**Excluded from the focus:** branded prompts (track separately only if asked), jobs to be done prompts, and any \"free\" modifier query. Do not build these unless requested.\n\n---\n\n## The workflow\n\n### Step 1: Gather the three inputs and the locale\nConfirm the website, the market and locale (localise spelling, currency, and local intent), and the verified competitor set (used here to read the third party citation landscape, not to write branded prompts). Take stock of the data; if none is provided, pull it (Ahrefs Keywords Explorer or Brand Radar, Semrush Keyword Magic) or proceed from the site pages and the sourcing channels, flagging the set as modelled until re anchored to real data.\n\n### Step 2: Build the topic list from the real service surface area\nDerive a fixed list of 8 to 12 topics from the client's service and product pages. This is a permanent axis. Distribute prompts across it, weighted by revenue priority if named.\n\n### Step 3: Source and filter to non branded, then split commercial from informational\nRead `references/data-to-prompt-pipeline.md`. Work the six sourcing channels. Filter hard to non branded, dropping branded, jobs, and free queries. Label each survivor commercial (goes to Campaign 1 or 2) or informational (goes to Campaign 3).\n\n### Step 4: Assign every commercial prompt to Citation Commercial or Mentions\nRead `references/campaigns.md`. For each commercial prompt, decide out create or pitch using the live citation landscape (what the engines cite for the query now) and the query shape heuristic (broad best X head terms usually pitch, so Campaign 2; specific use case, pricing, comparison, and verification queries usually out create, so Campaign 1). Do not impose a fixed ratio; discover it. Flag prompts that need both.\n\n### Step 5: Reshape, set qualifier depth, and populate the distribution\nRead `references/prompt-engineering.md` and `references/architecture-and-dedup.md`. Reshape keywords into conversational prompts (the longer the original query, the less you change it). Raise qualifier density on the more specific commercial prompts. Populate the default distribution: for a set of 100, roughly 40 Citation Commercial, 25 Mentions, and 35 Citation Information, then adjust to the real citation landscape.\n\n### Step 6: Deduplicate\nRead the deduplication protocol in `references/architecture-and-dedup.md`. One canonical prompt per matrix cell; a semantic distance check; cluster near duplicates and track only the canonical one. Tag every prompt with its campaign.\n\n### Step 7: Deliver, organised by campaign\nRead `references/running-and-tracking.md`. Deliver the set as three clearly labelled campaign sections, in priority order, each opening with its one line goal and its how to win method, then its prompts as a table (columns: number, prompt, topic, and a commercial weight for the commercial campaigns), one prompt per line for pasting into a tracker. Add the tracking setup notes and, per campaign, the execution shortlist: Citation Commercial and Citation Information prompts route into `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst` then the content writer; Mentions prompts route into the outreach playbook via `seo-geo-expert` and `seo-geo-linking-expert`. Run the Rule 0 dash scan before returning.\n\n---\n\n## What makes the set worth tracking (one-screen summary)\n\n- **Three campaigns, one order.** Citation Commercial first (leads), Mentions second (outreach), Citation Information third (the low hanging foundation that feeds both). The campaign name is the whole classification.\n- **Out create or pitch is the core call.** Can the client win this with their own page, or only by getting onto someone else's. Most broad commercial category queries are pitch (third party), so they belong to the Mentions campaign, not to content work.\n- **Never build a self serving best of page.** A brand's own best X page that ranks itself first gets cited without the recommendation, loses organic visibility, and carries FTC risk. Win category queries by earning independent inclusion, which is the Mentions campaign.\n- **Citation Information is the easiest and it compounds.** Owned explainer content builds citation precedence, so the brand is already trusted when the commercial question is asked. Run it in parallel.\n- **This mirrors the tools.** Content and AEO versus PR and Communications (Profound), out create versus outreach, citation versus mention. Naming the campaigns this way makes the output legible to any client and portable to any tracker.\n\n---\n\n## Reference files\n- **`references/campaigns.md`** The three campaigns in full: the out create versus pitch decision, the signals and query shape heuristic, the execution method per campaign, the citation versus mention metric split, how the major tools frame this, and the self serving listicle warning. Read at Step 4.\n- **`references/architecture-and-dedup.md`** The matrix, the three campaign priority model, the buckets, the recommended distribution, and the deduplication protocol.\n- **`references/prompt-engineering.md`** How a tracked prompt differs from a keyword: anatomy, the reshape rule, qualifier density, and per engine design.\n- **`references/data-to-prompt-pipeline.md`** The six sourcing channels, the intent taxonomy, Search Console regex, and the non branded filter.\n- **`references/running-and-tracking.md`** Loading the set into a tracker and the check maths, tagging and cadence, reading the baseline, routing the three campaigns to their execution, re measuring, and adapting to any account.\n- **`assets/prompt-set-template.md`** A blank template plus a fully worked non branded example, organised into the three campaigns, for a UK accounting outsourcing firm.\n\n---\n\n## Operating principles\n- **The output is three named campaigns, not codes.** Deliver Citation Commercial, Mentions, and Citation Information as labelled sections, each with its goal and its how to win. Never hand back opaque tags.\n- **Never advise a self serving best of page.** It is cited without recommendation, loses organic visibility, and carries FTC Consumer Review Rule risk. Win category queries through the Mentions campaign.\n- **Read the citation landscape before deciding out create or pitch.** Base the Campaign 1 versus Campaign 2 call on what the engines actually cite now, not assumption. If live AI data cannot be pulled, say so and ask the user to run the prompts or use an AI visibility tool.\n- **Anchor to real demand, reshape, do not fabricate.** Base prompts on queries people actually search; use AI generation only when real world queries run out.\n- **Hand off by campaign.** Citation Commercial and Citation Information go to `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst` then the content writer. Mentions goes to the outreach playbook via `seo-geo-expert` and `seo-geo-linking-expert`. Brand or site level problems route to `seo-geo-expert`.\n- **Treat tool percentages as directional.** AI responses are volatile; track clusters for a directional aggregate. The durable anchors are the three campaign structure, the out create versus pitch call, and citation precedence.\n\n\n===== FILE: references/campaigns.md =====\n# The Three Campaigns and the Out Create versus Pitch Decision\n\nThis is the core of the skill. Every prompt goes into one of three campaigns. The campaign is the classification, the priority, and the action. Read this at Step 4.\n\n## Table of contents\n1. Citation versus mention (the metric split the tools use)\n2. The three campaigns\n3. The out create versus pitch decision\n4. Signals and the query shape heuristic\n5. Execution method per campaign\n6. Prompts that need both\n7. The self serving listicle warning\n8. How the major tools frame this\n\n---\n\n## 1. Citation versus mention (the metric split the tools use)\n\nTwo different things get measured in an AI answer, and the whole model rests on the difference:\n\n- A **citation** is when the answer links a page as a source. If that page is the client's own, the client owns the citation.\n- A **mention** is when the client's brand name appears in the answer text, usually because a third party page that got cited names the client.\n\nAhrefs Brand Radar and Profound both track these as separate metrics. The strategic point: you win a citation by owning the cited page, and you win a mention by getting named on someone else's cited page. Those are two different jobs, which is why there are two commercial campaigns.\n\n## 2. The three campaigns\n\n- **Campaign 1, Citation Commercial (priority one).** Non branded commercial prompts the client can win with their own page, so the client's URL is the cited source. Goal: the citation and the lead. Method: out create (publish or optimise owned pages).\n- **Campaign 2, Mentions (priority two).** Non branded commercial prompts whose answer is built from third party sources, so an owned page cannot win. Goal: a brand mention on the pages the engine already cites. Method: pitch (outreach).\n- **Campaign 3, Citation Information (priority three, the low hanging foundation).** Non branded informational prompts the client wins with owned explainer content. Goal: own the cited explainer pages, build discoverability and citation precedence that feed Campaigns 1 and 2. Method: out create.\n\nInformational prompts always go to Campaign 3. The only judgement is splitting commercial prompts between Campaign 1 and Campaign 2.\n\n## 3. The out create versus pitch decision\n\nFor each commercial prompt, ask one question: can the client win this answer by publishing their own page, or only by getting onto someone else's. That is out create versus pitch, and it decides Campaign 1 versus Campaign 2. Work it in this order:\n\n1. **Read the citation landscape.** Run or look up what the engines cite for the query now. If the answer is built from vendor and service pages, or the query is narrow and factual, the client can out create it: Campaign 1. If the answer is built from independent listicles, directories, and forums, the client can only pitch: Campaign 2. This evidence overrides the heuristic.\n2. **Apply the query shape heuristic when live data is thin** (section 4).\n3. **Default broad category head terms to Campaign 2.** Around 85 to 93 percent of commercial category citations are third party, and the ranked best of listicle is the single most cited content format. Do not assume an owned page will win a broad best X term.\n\nRecord the campaign on every prompt. It drives the delivery and the routing.\n\n## 4. Signals and the query shape heuristic\n\n**Campaign 1, out create (owned page can win):**\n- The live citations already include vendor, service, pricing, use case, comparison, or FAQ pages.\n- The query is specific, qualified, long tail, or verification stage: pricing, integrations, security, best fit use case, \"how does X work for [use case]\", \"X vs Y\" as a considered comparison.\n- The answer intent is a factual or spec answer a first party page can authoritatively give.\n\n**Campaign 2, pitch (only outreach can win):**\n- The live citations are dominated by third party publishers, directories, and forums: best of listicles, comparison roundups, G2, Clutch, Capterra, Trustpilot, Reddit, Quora, YouTube, industry press.\n- The query is a broad category head term: \"best X\", \"top X in [place]\", \"best X for [broad segment]\".\n- The answer names a shortlist of vendors and the buyer expects an independent, cross vendor view.\n\n## 5. Execution method per campaign\n\n- **Citation Commercial (out create):** route into `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst` one query at a time. It returns a brief; `seo-geo-content-writer-expert` builds or optimises the owned page (pricing, use case, comparison, FAQ, service); `seo-geo-linking-expert` links it into the cluster. Add citations, statistics, and sourced claims to the page, the single most effective on page GEO tactic in the research.\n- **Mentions (pitch):** route into the outreach playbook via `seo-geo-expert` and `seo-geo-linking-expert`. From the baseline, list the exact third party pages cited for each query, then earn inclusion in the best of listicles and comparison roundups (pitch the publishers), claim and earn genuine reviews on the directories that lift citation odds (profiles on G2, Clutch, Capterra, and Trustpilot correlate with markedly higher citation probability), and participate authentically in the Reddit and Quora threads the engines pull from, plus digital PR for expert insight in industry press. Aim to appear in the first few entries of any list, because most cited brands sit in the top positions.\n- **Citation Information (out create):** route into `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst` then the content writer, same as Campaign 1, but for explainer and definitional pages. These are the easiest wins and build the foundation, so run them in parallel.\n\n## 6. Prompts that need both\n\nBecause of the citation versus mention divide, some commercial prompts need an owned page to state the facts cleanly and third party placement to earn the recommendation. Put these in both Campaign 1 and Campaign 2 and label them plainly as running in both. Do not invent a fourth category; just note the overlap.\n\n## 7. The self serving listicle warning\n\nDo not advise the client to publish their own best of page ranking themselves first to win a Campaign 2 query. Self promotional listicles get cited but recommend competitors most of the time (around 69 percent in one study of 100 B2B best category queries), and brands that leaned on self ranked best pages lost 30 to 50 percent of visibility, with declines accelerating through core updates. There is also FTC Consumer Review Rule exposure when company controlled content is presented as independent. The correct move on a category term is the Mentions campaign: earn inclusion on genuinely independent lists.\n\n## 8. How the major tools frame this\n\nThe three campaign split is not invented here; it mirrors the category:\n\n- **Profound** separates a Content and AEO layer (on site, out create) from a PR and Communications layer branded \"shape AI conversations\" (off site, pitch), and tracks citations and mentions as distinct metrics.\n- **Trackers** frame every cited source as \"outreach or outrank\": pitch the author or out create the page, based on the source's authority.\n- **Action lists** across the tools converge on three action types: create AI optimised content, earn mentions on the sources AI already trusts, and join community conversations like Reddit. Campaign 1 and Campaign 3 are the first; Campaign 2 is the second and third.\n- **Ahrefs Brand Radar** exposes cited pages and domains per prompt, which is the raw material for deciding out create versus pitch and for building the Mentions target list.\n\nNaming the campaigns this way keeps the output legible to any client and portable to any of these tools.\n\n\n===== FILE: references/architecture-and-dedup.md =====\n# Architecture, Distribution, and Deduplication\n\nThe three campaign priority model, the matrix that structures the set, the distribution that weights it, and the protocol that deduplicates it. Read before Steps 5 and 6. The out create versus pitch decision that assigns campaigns lives in `campaigns.md`; read that at Step 4 first.\n\n## Table of contents\n1. The three campaign priority model\n2. The matrix\n3. Buckets within each campaign\n4. The recommended distribution\n5. The deduplication protocol\n\n---\n\n## 1. The three campaign priority model\n\nEvery prompt belongs to exactly one campaign. Build in this order:\n\n- **Campaign 1, Citation Commercial (primary).** Commercial prompts won on the client's own pages (out create). The lead engine.\n- **Campaign 2, Mentions (second).** Commercial prompts won only by outreach onto third party sources (pitch). The outreach engine.\n- **Campaign 3, Citation Information (third, the low hanging foundation).** Informational prompts won on the client's own explainer pages (out create). Feeds the two commercial campaigns.\n\nExcluded from the focus: branded prompts (track separately only if asked), jobs to be done prompts, and any \"free\" modifier query.\n\n## 2. The matrix\n\nEvery prompt is one cell in a matrix. Deduplication falls out of it: two prompts in the same cell are duplicates.\n\n```\nCAMPAIGN         x   BUCKET                x   TOPIC             x   QUALIFIER DEPTH\n(3 campaigns)        (commercial or            (8 to 12)             (low, med, high)\n                      informational buckets)\n```\n\n## 3. Buckets within each campaign\n\n**Campaign 1 and Campaign 2 share the commercial buckets** (the campaign each prompt lands in is decided by out create versus pitch, not by bucket, though category head terms skew to Campaign 2 and specifics skew to Campaign 1):\n\n- **Category exploration:** \"best X\", \"top X in [place]\", \"best X for [use case or segment]\". Usually Campaign 2.\n- **Comparison of approaches:** \"X vs Y\" as categories, \"specialist vs full service\", \"in house vs agency\", \"alternatives to [category]\". Usually Campaign 1.\n- **Use case and segment fit:** \"best X for B2B\", \"X for [industry]\". Usually Campaign 1.\n- **Selection criteria:** \"how to choose X\", \"what to look for in an X\". Usually Campaign 1.\n- **Pricing:** \"how much does X cost\", \"typical X pricing in [market]\". Usually Campaign 1.\n- **Transactional and local:** \"X provider near me in [place]\", \"hire an X in [place]\", \"where to get an X quote\". Mixed, often both.\n\n**Campaign 3 uses the informational buckets:**\n\n- **Definitions:** \"what is X\", \"what does an X do\".\n- **Differences:** \"difference between X and Y\" as concepts.\n- **How and why:** \"how does X work\", \"why do businesses need X\", \"why is [problem] happening\".\n\n## 4. The recommended distribution\n\nLead with the commercial campaigns, support with the information campaign. For a set of 100:\n\n| Campaign | Prompts | Share | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Citation Commercial (out create) | 40 | 40 percent | Owned page winnable commercial prompts |\n| Mentions (pitch) | 25 | 25 percent | Third party dominated commercial prompts |\n| Citation Information (out create) | 35 | 35 percent | Low hanging owned explainer prompts, the foundation |\n| Total | 100 | 100 percent | |\n\nThis is a starting point, not a rule. The real split between Campaign 1 and Campaign 2 comes from the citation landscape: in categories where the data shows heavy third party dominance, Campaign 2 grows and Campaign 1 shrinks. Scale proportionally for other sizes (a 60 prompt set is 24, 15, 21).\n\nA workable spread of commercial buckets across Campaigns 1 and 2: Category around 18 (mostly Campaign 2), Comparison around 12, Use case around 12, Selection around 8, Pricing around 9, Transactional around 6, all mostly Campaign 1.\n\n## 5. The deduplication protocol\n\nEvery prompt must earn its tracking slot by returning a meaningfully different answer. Four rules:\n\n1. **One canonical prompt per matrix cell.** \"Best accounting outsourcing companies UK\" and \"top accounting outsourcing firms in the UK\" occupy the same cell. Keep one; the other is a variant.\n2. **Semantic distance check.** Two prompts are duplicates if they would return the same source set and brand set. Before adding a prompt, confirm it pulls a genuinely different answer, engine behaviour, or citation source than one already in the set. If not, drop it.\n3. **Cluster, track the canonical, spot check the variants.** Keep near duplicates as a labelled cluster; track the canonical one continuously; run the variants occasionally to confirm stability. This protects against response volatility (the same prompt returns different brands minute to minute).\n4. **Tag the campaign.** Tag every prompt Citation Commercial, Mentions, or Citation Information (and flag the few that run in both commercial campaigns). This is what lets you report and route by campaign. Branded prompts are excluded from the core set; if branded tracking is added later, keep it in a separate report so it never contaminates category share of voice.\n\n\n===== FILE: references/prompt-engineering.md =====\n# Prompt Engineering for GEO: a tracked prompt is not a keyword\n\nThis file explains what object you are building and how to shape it. Read it before Step 4.\n\n## Table of contents\n1. Keyword versus prompt\n2. The four part anatomy of a tracking prompt\n3. Qualifier density: the strongest lever\n4. The reshape rule\n5. Designing for each engine\n\n---\n\n## 1. Keyword versus prompt\n\nA keyword is a compressed, telegraphic string typed into a search box: \"payroll outsourcing uk\". A prompt is the fuller, natural question the same person asks a chatbot: \"we are a 12 person accountancy practice in the UK and payroll is eating our junior staff time, is it worth outsourcing payroll and what should we watch out for\". Conversing with AI is unlike traditional search: requests are more detailed, carry more constraints, and often sit inside a longer conversation. If you track raw keywords you are not measuring how anyone actually queries AI. Always translate.\n\n## 2. The four part anatomy of a tracking prompt\n\nA well built prompt has up to four parts. Which parts you include depends on funnel stage.\n\n1. **The core question.** The irreducible thing being asked. \"Is outsourcing bookkeeping worth it.\"\n2. **Contextual qualifiers.** The constraints and descriptors a buyer adds: persona (role, industry, business model), company size, constraints (budget, security, compliance, timeline, team skill), and tool stack (must work with Xero, integrates with our practice management software). This is the strongest lever on how narrow the answer becomes.\n3. **A recency cue where useful.** Adding \"in 2026\" or a current year nudges recency weighted engines (Perplexity most of all) to run a live web search and surface cited URLs, rather than answering from training data. Use it deliberately on prompts where the cited pages matter.\n4. **The intent verb.** \"Best\", \"top\", \"compare\", \"vs\", \"alternatives to\", \"reviews of\", \"how much does\", \"is X worth it\", \"should I\". These verbs class the prompt as commercial or transactional and make it decision influencing.\n\nTop of funnel prompts often carry only part 1. Bottom of funnel prompts carry all four.\n\n## 3. Qualifier density: the strongest lever\n\nQualifier density is how many constraints a prompt carries. It rises with buyer specificity, and it correlates with the campaign. Target roughly:\n\n| Prompt type | Qualifier depth | Qualifier focus |\n|---|---|---|\n| Citation Information (informational) | Minimal, mostly unqualified | Persona only |\n| Broad category commercial (usually Mentions) | Low to moderate | Market and segment |\n| Specific commercial, use case, pricing, comparison, selection (usually Citation Commercial) | Higher | Company size, constraints, tool stack |\n\nA generic prompt returns broad category answers; a highly qualified prompt narrows the recommended set sharply. Both are useful: the generic category ones measure category presence and are usually Mentions campaign (pitch) territory, while the qualified specific ones measure whether you win the exact buyer you want and are usually Citation Commercial (out create) territory.\n\n## 4. The reshape rule\n\nConvert keywords to prompts without distorting intent using one rule: **the longer the original query, the less you change it; short head terms need more conversational expansion, long tail terms often already carry the intent.**\n\n- Head term \"accounting outsourcing\" becomes a full qualified question (\"what is accounting outsourcing and how does it work for a UK practice\").\n- Long tail \"is outsourcing payroll to india gdpr compliant\" is left almost as is, because it already reads like a prompt.\n\nBlend short and long, generic and qualifier heavy, keyword native and forum native, so the set reflects the real spread of how people ask.\n\n## 5. Designing for each engine\n\nThe engines behave differently, so the set must exercise all of them.\n\n- **ChatGPT** leans on consensus, encyclopedic, and major publication sources, with Wikipedia its single most cited domain. It cites less often but extracts deeply. Rewards definitional and category prompts where a canonical answer exists. Note that some ChatGPT prompts answer from training data and skip web search; a year cue helps force retrieval when you care about cited URLs.\n- **Perplexity** searches live on every query, always cites, and weights freshness heavily, leaning on Reddit, YouTube, and review sites. Rewards comparison, reviews, best of, and anything with a year cue.\n- **Claude** favours structured, analytical, comparative content and cites bullet pointed and research style pages over promotional ones. Rewards frameworks, criteria based comparisons, and how to workflows.\n- **Google AI Overviews and AI Mode** (Gemini powered) extract passages and weight E-E-A-T heavily, with AI Mode pulling an even wider, more user generated pool. Rewards high volume informational and commercial questions with strong People Also Ask clusters.\n\nThe mention versus source divide runs through all of them: when an engine recommends a brand it usually cites a third party page (a listing site, a Reddit thread, industry press), not the brand's own site. So a rival can win the recommendation while a review site wins the link. This is exactly why every non branded commercial prompt is sorted into either the Citation Commercial campaign (out create, win the citation on your own page) or the Mentions campaign (pitch, earn the mention on the cited third party page); see `campaigns.md`. Broad category prompts are Mentions territory, while specific, use case, pricing, and verification prompts are where an owned page can win. Brand mention frequency across authoritative third party sources predicts AI citation more strongly than backlinks do.\n\n\n===== FILE: references/data-to-prompt-pipeline.md =====\n# The Data to Prompt Pipeline\n\nThis is the repeatable engine that converts the client's data into raw, real world queries ready to reshape. Read it before Step 3.\n\n## Table of contents\n1. What the data actually does\n2. The six sourcing channels\n3. The Semrush intent taxonomy\n4. Search Console regex helpers\n5. The five step build\n\n---\n\n## 1. What the data actually does\n\nKeyword exports, SEO metrics, and rank data do three jobs:\n\n- **Sourcing and prioritisation.** Search volume, keyword difficulty, and clicks tell you which topics have proven demand, so you anchor prompts to real interest rather than guessing what people ask.\n- **Intent labelling.** Semrush and Ahrefs both tag keywords by intent. That label pre sorts raw keywords into the funnel buckets, so you do not classify by hand.\n- **Gap evidence.** Positions, cited pages, and share of voice by competitor tell you which queries are winnable and where a weak third party source currently owns the citation. This feeds prioritisation of which prompts to hand to the analyst skill first.\n\nIf no data is provided, pull it (Ahrefs Keywords Explorer or Brand Radar, Semrush Keyword Magic) or proceed from the site pages and the channels below, and flag that the set is modelled and should be re anchored to real data.\n\n## 2. The six sourcing channels (priority order)\n\n1. **Own keyword data (highest trust).** Ahrefs Keywords Explorer, Questions tab; Semrush Keyword Magic filtered by Questions and Intent; Search Console queries the site already ranks for. Proven queries carry real demand and are your anchor.\n2. **People Also Ask and AI Overviews.** If Google already wraps a topic in a PAA cluster or an AI Overview, that topic is answer friendly and likely to matter in AI discovery. Harvest PAA questions as subtopics and near ready prompts.\n3. **Reddit, Quora, and forums.** These surface the messy, high context way buyers phrase things, including the constraints and frustrations pure keyword data strips out. This is where the qualifier rich, long form prompts come from.\n4. **Competitor pages.** Reverse engineer rivals' comparison pages, service pages, and FAQs to see which decision questions are in play in the category.\n5. **LLM native expansion.** Use Perplexity follow up suggestions, or ask an engine \"what do people ask when researching [topic]\", to generate conversational variants of a root question and add diversity.\n6. **AI generation (last resort only).** Only when real world queries run out, typically for niche topics with thin keyword data. Never let synthetic prompts dominate.\n\n## 3. The Semrush intent taxonomy\n\nLabel every raw query with one primary intent. This is the standard four class model used by Semrush and Ahrefs.\n\n- **Informational:** wants to learn. Words like what, why, how, guide. Low immediate buyer intent, high value for entity association.\n- **Navigational:** wants a specific brand or page. Usually branded. Converts at a high rate when it is the client's brand.\n- **Commercial:** researching options before a purchase. Words like best, top, vs, alternatives, reviews. High buyer intent; this is where AI names vendors.\n- **Transactional:** ready to act. Words like buy, hire, quote, book a demo, pricing. Highest intent, lowest volume.\n\nCommercial and transactional queries drive the large majority of revenue relevant traffic in classic search (Semrush data puts commercial intent at around 72 percent of monthly organic visits for major retail domains), and in AI search they are the prompts where a vendor shortlist is actually named. Weight the set accordingly.\n\n**Filter to non branded, then to two intents.** This skill builds a non branded set. Drop navigational and branded queries from the core set (track branded separately only if asked). Fold transactional non branded queries into the commercial group. Then keep only two intents: commercial (which becomes the Citation Commercial and Mentions campaigns) and informational (which becomes the Citation Information campaign). Also drop jobs to be done queries and any query with a \"free\" modifier: they attract non buyers and dilute the commercial signal.\n\n## 4. Search Console regex helpers\n\nWhen mining Search Console for real queries the site already ranks for:\n\n- Isolate question queries: `\\b(why|what|when|are|will|does|should|where|who|how|can|do|is)\\b`\n- Isolate longer conversational queries (six words or more): `^(\\S+\\s+){5}\\S+` (raise the 5 for longer minimums)\n\nThese extract the queries most likely to translate cleanly into prompts.\n\n## 5. The five step build\n\n1. **Filter to non branded, then split commercial from informational first, not keywords.** Sort everything into commercial and informational before touching keywords, dropping branded, jobs, and free queries. This keeps the set commercial led.\n2. **Build the buckets** (full taxonomy in `architecture-and-dedup.md`). This guarantees you are not tracking fifty best X prompts and nothing else.\n3. **Assign each commercial prompt to Citation Commercial or Mentions** with the out create versus pitch decision (see `campaigns.md`), from the citation landscape; informational prompts go to Citation Information. This is what makes the set actionable rather than just measurable.\n4. **Assign topics.** Map the client's real service and product surface area to the fixed topic list, then distribute prompts (evenly or by revenue priority) across topics within each campaign.\n5. **Populate and set qualifier density,** reshaping per the reshape rule, blending short and long, generic and qualifier heavy, keyword native and forum native. Raise qualifier density on the more specific commercial prompts (they are the ones most likely to be Citation Commercial).\n\n\n===== FILE: references/running-and-tracking.md =====\n# Running the Set, Tracking, and Handoff\n\nThis file covers what happens after the set is built: loading it into a tracker, reading the baseline, routing the three campaigns to execution, re measuring, and adapting the machine to any account. Read it before Step 7.\n\n## Table of contents\n1. The order of operations on an account\n2. Loading into a tracker\n3. The check maths and cadence\n4. Reading the baseline\n5. Routing the three campaigns to execution\n6. Re measuring\n7. Adapting to any account\n\n---\n\n## 1. The order of operations on an account\n\n```\n1. Pull the data          -> keyword export + intent tags + competitor domains\n2. Build the prompt set   -> this skill: three campaigns, deduplicated\n3. Load into a tracker    -> Brand Radar custom prompts, one per line, tagged by campaign\n4. Read the baseline      -> where you appear, where rivals appear and you do not\n5. Route by campaign      -> Citation Commercial + Citation Information to\n                             seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst (out create the page),\n                             Mentions to the outreach playbook (pitch for placement)\n6. Execute                -> content-writer + linking + expert skills close the gap\n7. Re-measure             -> same set, monthly cadence, watch share of voice by campaign\n```\n\nThe prompt set is both the tracking instrument and the plan the three campaigns work through.\n\n## 2. Loading into a tracker\n\nPrimary path, Ahrefs Brand Radar custom prompts:\n\n1. Create a Brand Radar report with the client site as the brand and the verified competitor list. Brand Radar will also auto suggest associated competitors as a cross check.\n2. Open Tracked prompts and paste the set, one prompt per line.\n3. Select locations (the client market or markets) and the LLMs to check (ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity, Copilot, and the Google AI surfaces).\n4. Apply tags: campaign (Citation Commercial, Mentions, or Citation Information), topic, and a both flag for the few commercial prompts that run in both commercial campaigns. Tagging is what lets you pull aggregate share of voice by campaign and topic, rather than drowning in individual, volatile results.\n\nAlternatives that also take a pasted prompt list: Semrush AI toolkit, Profound, Peec, Otterly, Frase AI Visibility. The set is tool agnostic. The one per line format pastes into any of them, and the campaign structure maps onto Profound's Content and AEO versus PR and Communications layers directly.\n\n## 3. The check maths and cadence\n\nIn Brand Radar, one check equals one prompt times one LLM times one location. So 100 prompts across 5 engines in 1 location is 500 checks per run; across 3 locations it is 1,500. Budget accordingly and set frequency to fit the allowance. Monthly is usually the right cadence for a stable read, because AI ranking is volatile minute to minute and you want a directional aggregate, not a live feed. Reduce frequency to stretch checks and track more prompts at once.\n\n## 4. Reading the baseline\n\nFilter for prompts where a competitor is named and the client is not: those are the priority gaps. Cross reference the cited pages, because this is what decides out create versus pitch: if the cited pages are vendor pages, it is a Citation Commercial gap you out create; if they are third party listicles, directories, or forums, it is a Mentions gap you pitch for. Remember most citations about a brand come from third party sites, so the Mentions target list includes pages the client does not own (a listing to get onto, a Reddit thread to join, an industry page to earn a mention on).\n\n## 5. Routing the three campaigns to execution\n\n**Citation Commercial and Citation Information** go one query at a time into `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst`. That skill scans the Google top 10 and the AI citation landscape for the exact query, runs its five axis gap analysis, chooses an information gain angle, and returns a brief. Then `seo-geo-content-writer-expert` drafts and optimises the owned page (a commercial page for Campaign 1, an explainer page for Campaign 3), and `seo-geo-linking-expert` handles internal and external links. Carry two fields forward on each: search intent (the query to appear for) and answer intent (the exact extractable answer that wins the citation). Add citations, statistics, and sourced claims to the page, the single most effective on page GEO tactic in the research.\n\n**Mentions** goes into the outreach playbook via `seo-geo-expert` and `seo-geo-linking-expert`. From the baseline, list the exact third party pages the engines cite for each Mentions query, then work them: earn inclusion in the ranking best of listicles and comparison roundups (pitch the publishers), claim and earn genuine reviews on the directories that lift citation odds (G2, Clutch, Capterra, Trustpilot), participate authentically in the Reddit and Quora threads the engines pull from, and run digital PR for expert insight mentions in industry press. Aim for the first few entries of any list, because most cited brands sit in the top positions. Never answer a Mentions query by publishing a self serving best of page; it gets cited without the recommendation and carries FTC risk.\n\nPrompts flagged as running in both commercial campaigns go to both routes: an owned page to state the facts, third party placement to earn the recommendation. Brand or site level problems (thin cluster, missing pillar, weak overall share of voice) route to `seo-geo-expert`.\n\n## 6. Re measuring\n\nRun the same set monthly. Because the set is controlled and deduplicated, movement in share of voice is attributable, not noise. Report by campaign and topic so the client sees each engine working: whether the Citation Commercial pages are earning citations, whether the Mentions outreach is getting the brand onto the cited third party pages, and whether the Citation Information foundation is growing discoverability.\n\n## 7. Adapting to any account\n\nThe structure is fixed; the content is swappable. To rebuild for any client:\n\n1. Swap the topic list for the client's real service and product surface area.\n2. Pull the client's keyword export and let intent tags pre sort commercial from informational.\n3. Reset the competitor slots to the client's verified rivals (used to read the citation landscape).\n4. Keep the campaign order (Citation Commercial, Mentions, Citation Information) and decide out create versus pitch for every commercial prompt from that account's own citation landscape. The 40 / 25 / 35 default is a starting point; let the citation data move it.\n5. Localise qualifiers and spelling to the target market (US, UK, Indian, Australian, Canadian English), and add local intent (city or region prompts).\n6. Run the deduplication protocol: one canonical prompt per matrix cell, cluster the rest.\n\n\n===== FILE: assets/prompt-set-template.md =====\n# Prompt Set Template and Worked Example\n\nCopy the blank template, fill it for the client, then check it against the worked example. The set is non branded and organised into three campaigns in priority order. The campaign is the classification: no other codes.\n\n## Blank template\n\nDeliver three labelled sections, each opening with its goal and its how to win, then a table. Columns: number, prompt, topic (1 to N), weight (L, M, H).\n\n```\n## Campaign 1: Citation Commercial (out create)  -> win with your own page, earn the citation and the lead\n| # | Prompt | Topic | Wt |\n|---|--------|-------|----|\n\n## Campaign 2: Mentions (pitch)  -> win by outreach onto the third party sources AI cites\n| # | Prompt | Topic | Wt |\n\n## Campaign 3: Citation Information (out create)  -> low hanging owned explainers that feed the two above\n| # | Prompt | Topic | Wt |\n```\n\nDefault for a set of 100: roughly 40 Citation Commercial, 25 Mentions, 35 Citation Information, then adjust to the citation landscape.\n\n---\n\n## Worked example: UK accounting outsourcing firm\n\nVertical: a UK accounting outsourcing firm serving accountancy practices and SMEs. Non branded only. Campaign assignment is illustrative and should be confirmed against the live citation landscape. 45 prompts shown; scale to 100 by adding to each campaign.\n\nTopics: 1 Accounting outsourcing (general and offshore), 2 Bookkeeping outsourcing, 3 Payroll outsourcing, 4 Audit support and outsourcing, 5 Year end and management accounts, 6 VAT and Making Tax Digital, 7 Corporation tax and self assessment, 8 Cloud accounting software support, 9 White label outsourcing, 10 Data security, GDPR, and offshore compliance.\n\n### Campaign 1: Citation Commercial (out create)\n\nGoal: the client's own page is the cited source on a commercial query, which drives the lead. How to win: build or optimise owned comparison, use case, selection, and pricing pages, answer first, with sourced data. Route into `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst` then the content writer.\n\n| # | Prompt | Topic | Wt |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Offshore India team vs UK based outsourcing for accounts, which is safer | 10 | H |\n| 2 | Specialist accounting outsourcer vs full service provider | 1 | M |\n| 3 | In house team vs an outsourcing partner for a UK practice | 1 | H |\n| 4 | Outsourced bookkeeping vs hiring a freelance bookkeeper | 2 | H |\n| 5 | Outsourcing compliance work vs hiring more junior accountants | 1 | H |\n| 6 | Accounting outsourcing for a UK practice that runs on Xero | 8 | H |\n| 7 | Payroll outsourcing for a firm running fifty or more payrolls | 3 | H |\n| 8 | Audit support for a practice facing its first busy audit season | 4 | M |\n| 9 | White label bookkeeping for a growing UK practice | 9 | H |\n| 10 | Outsourcing corporation tax and self assessment for the January crunch | 7 | H |\n| 11 | How to choose an accounting outsourcing partner in the UK | 1 | H |\n| 12 | What to look for in an offshore accounting provider | 1 | M |\n| 13 | Questions to ask an accounting outsourcing company before signing | 1 | M |\n| 14 | How much does it cost to outsource bookkeeping in the UK | 2 | H |\n| 15 | What does accounting outsourcing cost per client in the UK | 1 | H |\n| 16 | How much does payroll outsourcing cost for a UK practice | 3 | H |\n| 17 | Typical pricing models for outsourced accounting, per hour vs per client vs FTE | 1 | M |\n| 18 | Where to get a quote for outsourced bookkeeping and payroll in the UK | 2 | H |\n\n### Campaign 2: Mentions (pitch)\n\nGoal: the client's brand is named on the third party pages AI cites for broad category queries, because an owned page cannot win these. How to win: read the cited pages, then earn inclusion on the best of listicles and directories (Clutch, GoodFirms, DesignRush, Sortlist), earn genuine reviews (G2, Capterra, Trustpilot), and participate in the Reddit and Quora threads and industry press. Route into the outreach playbook via `seo-geo-expert` and `seo-geo-linking-expert`. Never publish a self serving best of page.\n\n| # | Prompt | Topic | Wt |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 19 | Best accounting outsourcing companies for UK practices in 2026 | 1 | H |\n| 20 | Top bookkeeping outsourcing providers in the UK | 2 | H |\n| 21 | Best payroll outsourcing services for UK accountants | 3 | H |\n| 22 | Best audit support outsourcing firms for UK auditors | 4 | H |\n| 23 | Top white label accounting outsourcing companies in the UK | 9 | H |\n| 24 | Best offshore accounting providers for UK accountancy firms | 1 | H |\n| 25 | Best GDPR compliant offshore accounting providers for UK firms | 10 | H |\n| 26 | Best accounting outsourcing firms for small UK practices on a tight budget | 1 | H |\n| 27 | Top VAT and MTD outsourcing providers for UK accountants | 6 | H |\n| 28 | Which outsourcing companies specialise in Xero for UK practices | 8 | H |\n| 29 | Recommended year end accounts outsourcing partners in the UK | 5 | H |\n| 30 | Best accounting outsourcing firm for practices near Brighton or the South East (also runs in Citation Commercial for the local page) | 1 | H |\n\n### Campaign 3: Citation Information (out create)\n\nGoal: the client's own explainer page is cited on the questions buyers ask while learning, building the discoverability and citation precedence that feed the two commercial campaigns. How to win: publish owned explainer content, answer first, sourced, structured for extraction. The easiest wins; run in parallel. Route into `seo-geo-serp-gap-analyst` then the content writer.\n\n| # | Prompt | Topic | Wt |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 31 | What is accounting outsourcing and how does it work | 1 | L |\n| 32 | What is the difference between outsourcing and offshoring for accounts | 1 | L |\n| 33 | What does an outsourced bookkeeping service actually do | 2 | L |\n| 34 | What is payroll outsourcing | 3 | L |\n| 35 | What is audit outsourcing and is it allowed in the UK | 4 | L |\n| 36 | What are management accounts and why do businesses need them | 5 | L |\n| 37 | What is Making Tax Digital and who does it apply to | 6 | M |\n| 38 | What is cloud accounting and how is it different from desktop software | 8 | L |\n| 39 | What is white label accounting outsourcing for a practice | 9 | L |\n| 40 | Is offshore accounting GDPR compliant | 10 | M |\n| 41 | What is the difference between bookkeeping and accounting | 2 | L |\n| 42 | How does outsourced payroll handle pension auto enrolment | 3 | L |\n| 43 | What accounting tasks can safely be outsourced | 1 | M |\n| 44 | How does data security work when outsourcing accounts offshore | 10 | M |\n| 45 | Why do accountancy firms outsource work | 1 | L |\n\n### The read\n\n- **Three campaigns, in priority order,** each with a plain goal and a plain action. No opaque codes.\n- **Citation Commercial (1 to 18)** is the lead engine: owned comparison, use case, selection, and pricing pages. Priority to feed the analyst skill: 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 14, 15.\n- **Mentions (19 to 30)** is the outreach engine: these broad best of terms are third party dominated, so the work is getting onto the listicles and directories the engines cite, not writing a page. Confirm the exact cited pages per query before pitching.\n- **Citation Information (31 to 45)** is the low hanging foundation: the easiest to win, and it builds the citation precedence that makes the two commercial campaigns land. Run it in parallel.\n- **Prompt 30 runs in both** commercial campaigns: an owned local page plus placement on local directories.\n- **No two prompts share a matrix cell**, which is the deduplication guarantee.\n\n\n";
export default PROMPT_SET_ARCHITECT_BUNDLE;
